# Rank 1648 / CVE-2022-23517: rails-html-sanitizer's permit scrubber rewrote the
# SVG attributes that may carry a local reference with an unanchored global
# regex substitution, and the substitution's cost is quadratic in the length of
# the attacker-supplied attribute value (CWE-1333 inefficient regular expression
# complexity, CWE-400; GHSA-5x79-w82f-gw8w).
#
# `PermitScrubber#scrub_attribute` (lib/rails/html/scrubbers.rb:148-150 at the
# fix parent) reaches, for every attribute named in
# `Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ATTR_VAL_ALLOWS_REF` (`fill`, `stroke`,
# `filter`, `clip-path` and the rest),
# `attr_node.value = attr_node.value.gsub(/url\s*\(\s*[^#\s][^)]+?\)/m, " ")`.
# The pattern is unanchored and applied globally, so `gsub` retries it at every
# `url(` in the attribute value, and each retry grows `[^)]+?` one character at
# a time to the end of the value when no `)` follows -- a value built out of
# repeated `url(` prefixes with no closing parenthesis costs O(n^2). The
# attribute value is markup the caller handed the sanitizer, which is the input
# a sanitizer exists to accept, so the bound is entirely the attacker's.
#
# The pool row carries no flag, and 56c61c0cebd1e493e8ad7bca2a0191609a4a6979
# reads as the real, targeted fix ("fix: replace slow regex attribute check with
# Loofah method"): neither a merge nor a release bump, it touches
# lib/rails/html/scrubbers.rb plus a regression test, the vulnerable `gsub` is
# present at the parent, and the remediation is present at the commit itself.
# The `gsub` call is replaced by `Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.scrub_attribute_that_
# allows_local_ref`, which Loofah implements over Crass's component-value
# parser. No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, re-measured. A bounded scan of the fix parent under
# the library profile reports 0 findings and 0 flags over ruby:6, stable over
# three --cache off runs; the fixed revision is identical. lib/rails/html/
# scrubbers.rb is among the six parsed files, so the zero is real.
#
# The source half is present. The library profile's parameter source labels
# every method parameter `code.ExternalEntryInput`
# (bindings_apply.go paramSourceApplicator), and `core.UserControlledData` is
# that profile's entrypoint, so `scrub_attribute`'s `node` and `attr_node`
# params are sources: `vyql match -profile library` reports 19 param labels on
# the real file. Taint survives the `.value` read and any unresolved receiver
# chain (`value`, `value.foo`, `value.bar.baz` as a `case` subject all report).
# The first pass recorded "no source" because `vyql match` was run without the
# profile; that was an artifact of the invocation, not a gap.
#
# Two blockers remain, both on the sink side, and the pair below isolates each
# inside the real method rather than on a synthetic parameter.
#
# First, the pattern is not classified as backtracking-prone, and that
# classification is correct as far as it goes: `regexambig.Ambiguous` answers
# false for `url\s*\(\s*[^#\s][^)]+?\)` because the pattern has no overlapping
# quantified branches -- `\s*` is followed by `\(` and by `[^#\s]`, both
# disjoint from `\s`, and `[^)]+?` is followed by `\)`, which it excludes. The
# cost is polynomial in the number of start positions `gsub` retries, not
# exponential in one match attempt, and polynomial backtracking is a class the
# engine does not model. So the `regex` node lowers to a plain `nir.Const`
# instead of the `__regex.match` call that `rubyRegexMayBacktrack`
# (internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/ruby.go) synthesises for a
# backtracking-prone literal, and no `code.RegexMatch` or `code.RegexMatchSink`
# label is ever emitted: `regexMatch205` in vyql/bindings/ruby/m5a48bf47.vyql
# keys on that synthesised node.
#
# Second, a regex supplied as a call argument has no operand link. When
# `rubyRegexMayBacktrack` answers true, the regex node lowers to an
# `__regex.match` call whose only argument is the pattern text itself, so
# `value.gsub(/re/, " ")` produces a labelled sink no receiver can reach. The
# one route that links an operand is `case value when /re/`, which `rubyCase`
# lowers to a Switch carrying the case value as Subject and the regex as a
# Label. The pair measures both axes on the real method: the reportable
# pattern over `attr_node.value` reports VYQL-DOS-002 in the `case` form and
# reports nothing as a `gsub` argument, and reports nothing in the `case` form
# once the literal carries the real quadratic quantifiers.
#
# Third, measured while building that pair and new: a statement-level `if`
# modifier drops the `case` lowering. `rubyCase` is reached only from the
# statement dispatch's `case` arm, and `if_modifier`/`unless_modifier` have no
# arm there, so `case x when /re/ ... end if cond` falls through to the generic
# expression fallback, which flattens the whole construct into a `nir.Seq` of
# its children. Source and sink both stay labelled -- the `__regex.match` node
# still exists -- but the Switch that carries the subject-to-label edge never
# forms, so the taint path is gone. Minimal pair below, differing only in the
# trailing modifier: 1 -> 0, stable over three runs. The real code spells the
# modifier on the assignment carrying the `gsub`, so this third absence sits in
# front of the other two for any future sweep.
#
# Teaching the classifier polynomial backtracking is what a fix would mean,
# and the measurement that killed it stands: over the `lib/` trees of loofah,
# rack and sanitize -- 268 Ruby regex literals, all three libraries whose job
# is handling untrusted input -- every candidate pattern shape flags 1 real in
# 7 to 11, and rack's `/bytes=([^;]+)/` against the untrusted `Range` header is
# structurally identical to this pattern and harmless. The discriminator is not
# in the pattern; it is that the operand is unbounded attacker-supplied
# attribute value, and what the fixed revision changes is the algorithm that
# reads it. The parameter source makes that gate weaker rather than stronger:
# under the library profile every parameter is a source, so the taint rule
# alone separates nothing, and an operand-boundedness notion is still required.
# Keying instead on the Loofah call the fixed revision installs would name the
# fixing commit's own vocabulary, and a binding on "an attribute value read
# rewritten by a regex substitution" flags loofah's own CVE-2018-8048
# remediation, which is exactly that shape and is safe. No definition change.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1648: the permit scrubber substitutes the SVG ref attributes with a quadratic regex"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rails/html/scrubbers.rb
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-001
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  require "cgi"

  module Rails
    module Html
      class PermitScrubber < Loofah::Scrubber
        def scrub_attributes(node)
          node.attribute_nodes.each do |attr_node|
            scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
          end
        end

        def scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
          attr_name = if attr_node.namespace
                        "#{attr_node.namespace.prefix}:#{attr_node.node_name}"
                      else
                        attr_node.node_name
                      end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ATTR_VAL_IS_URI.include?(attr_name)
            val_unescaped = CGI.unescapeHTML(attr_node.value).gsub(Loofah::HTML5::Scrub::CONTROL_CHARACTERS, "").downcase
            if val_unescaped =~ /^[a-z0-9][-+.a-z0-9]*:/ && !Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ALLOWED_PROTOCOLS.include?(val_unescaped.split(Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::PROTOCOL_SEPARATOR)[0])
              attr_node.remove
            end
          end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ATTR_VAL_ALLOWS_REF.include?(attr_name)
            attr_node.value = attr_node.value.gsub(/url\s*\(\s*[^#\s][^)]+?\)/m, " ") if attr_node.value
          end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ALLOW_LOCAL_HREF.include?(node.name) && attr_name == "xlink:href" && attr_node.value =~ /^\s*[^#\s].*/m
            attr_node.remove
          end

          Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.force_correct_attribute_escaping! node
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1648: the permit scrubber handing the attribute to Loofah's component-value parser is clean"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rails/html/scrubbers.rb
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-001
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  require "cgi"

  module Rails
    module Html
      class PermitScrubber < Loofah::Scrubber
        def scrub_attributes(node)
          node.attribute_nodes.each do |attr_node|
            scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
          end
        end

        def scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
          attr_name = if attr_node.namespace
                        "#{attr_node.namespace.prefix}:#{attr_node.node_name}"
                      else
                        attr_node.node_name
                      end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ATTR_VAL_IS_URI.include?(attr_name)
            val_unescaped = CGI.unescapeHTML(attr_node.value).gsub(Loofah::HTML5::Scrub::CONTROL_CHARACTERS, "").downcase
            if val_unescaped =~ /^[a-z0-9][-+.a-z0-9]*:/ && !Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ALLOWED_PROTOCOLS.include?(val_unescaped.split(Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::PROTOCOL_SEPARATOR)[0])
              attr_node.remove
            end
          end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ATTR_VAL_ALLOWS_REF.include?(attr_name)
            Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.scrub_attribute_that_allows_local_ref(attr_node)
          end

          if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ALLOW_LOCAL_HREF.include?(node.name) && attr_name == "xlink:href" && attr_node.value =~ /^\s*[^#\s].*/m
            attr_node.remove
          end

          Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.force_correct_attribute_escaping! node
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

# The isolating pair, on the real method's own scope. Each block is the real
# `scrub_attribute` verbatim with only the SVG-ref branch's statement changed,
# so the source half (the library profile's parameter source, taint surviving
# the `.value` read) is identical across all three and each difference is one
# axis:
#   -- case form, reportable literal  -> reports. Proves source, classifier and
#      the Switch operand link all work inside this method.
#   -- gsub form, reportable literal  -> silent. Only the call form differs, so
#      the receiver-to-regex-argument edge is what is missing.
#   -- case form, the real literal    -> silent. Only the quantifiers differ, so
#      the cost model is what is missing.

test "rank1648 pair: the real scrub_attribute with a reportable literal in the case form"
  lang ruby
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  require "cgi"

  class Scrub
    def scrub_attribute(node, attr_node)
      attr_name = if attr_node.namespace
                    "#{attr_node.namespace.prefix}:#{attr_node.node_name}"
                  else
                    attr_node.node_name
                  end

      if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ATTR_VAL_IS_URI.include?(attr_name)
        val_unescaped = CGI.unescapeHTML(attr_node.value).gsub(Loofah::HTML5::Scrub::CONTROL_CHARACTERS, "").downcase
        if val_unescaped =~ /^[a-z0-9][-+.a-z0-9]*:/ && !Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::ALLOWED_PROTOCOLS.include?(val_unescaped.split(Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::PROTOCOL_SEPARATOR)[0])
          attr_node.remove
        end
      end
      if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ATTR_VAL_ALLOWS_REF.include?(attr_name)
        if attr_node.value
          case attr_node.value
          when /url\s*\(\s*([^#\s]+)+\)/m
            attr_node.value = " "
          end
        end
      end
      if Loofah::HTML5::SafeList::SVG_ALLOW_LOCAL_HREF.include?(node.name) && attr_name == "xlink:href" && attr_node.value =~ /^\s*[^#\s].*/m
        attr_node.remove
      end

      Loofah::HTML5::Scrub.force_correct_attribute_escaping! node
    end
  end
  ```

